SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Philippines ratifies the Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons (TPNW)","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"2021-02-18","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?clang=_en&objid=08000002805a27ef","claim":"UN treaty records show the Philippines ratified the TPNW in February 2021, establishing the baseline that Marcos inherited in 2022.","locator":"Action details rows 10-23","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN treaty registry with ratification metadata.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Inherited baseline entering 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Pre-Marcos state action; included only for baseline.","contrary_evidence":["This duplicates the UNODA ratification record and adds no Marcos-specific conduct."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary:
- I screened roughly 30 distinct documents across UN mission pages, treaty texts, IAEA records, GMA, PNA, Reuters syndications, official-government pages, and later reference pages.
- Blocked pages removed: the official SONA pages at `stateofthenation.gov.ph` and `pco.gov.ph` returned 403/429, so I used the accessible GMA transcript instead.
- Duplicate removals: the UNTC TPNW action page duplicates the UNODA ratification page; ICAN status pages duplicate the treaty-status baseline; several SONA syndications repeated the same presidential text.
- Out-of-period removals: 2023-2026 nuclear-energy and defense items, plus later 2024-2026 missive-style coverage, were dropped.
- Weak/irrelevant removals: South China Sea, general inflation, and general governance pieces were rejected unless they contained a direct nuclear or existential-risk choice.
- Overlap removals: some Reuters/Reuters-syndicated articles on nuclear energy were useful only as background, but the GMA/UN/PNA sources above were more direct and less duplicated.

Remaining gaps:
- There is no evidence of a Marcos-controlled nuclear arsenal, command-and-control system, or nuclear crisis, so 1B.3, 1B.5, 1B.6, and 1B.8 remain structurally thin by design.
- I found no 2022 ruler-specific decisions on cyber, biological, or AI catastrophic-risk governance that would materially bear on 1B.9 beyond the Philippine delegation's general warning about emerging technologies.
- More searching would only help if you want deeper treatment of civilian nuclear-energy regulation or cabinet-level records; it is unlikely to change the core conclusion that this is a limited-exposure, non-scored chapter for 2022.

