**New Evidence**
- `Gov’t agencies’ cash usage hits 94% at end of Nov.` from BusinessWorld adds a real execution signal, not just a plan: by end-November 2022, agencies had used 94 percent of the P4.01 trillion in NCAs issued, up from 91 percent a year earlier. This is not a Marcos-only metric, but it does show the administration’s spending machinery was actually moving funds.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Gov’t agencies’ cash usage hits 94% at end of Nov.","publisher":"BusinessWorld Online","publication_date":"2022-12-20","url":"https://www.bworldonline.com/economy/2022/12/20/494319/govt-agencies-cash-usage-hits-94-at-end-of-nov/","claim":"Government agencies had used 94% of the P4.01 trillion in Notices of Cash Allocation issued in the first 11 months of 2022, leaving P257.94 billion unused, up from 91% a year earlier.","locator":"opening paragraphs of the article","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent business reporting citing DBM data, with explicit figures and comparison to the prior year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"In-period; late-2022 execution metric for the Marcos administration.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Marcos through administration-level spending control, but also affected by inherited budget processes and election-ban timing.","contrary_evidence":["This is a cash-utilization measure, not a direct output or welfare outcome measure.","It does not isolate Marcos-specific agency performance from pre-existing spending momentum."],"lenses":["8B.6","8B.8","8B.10"]}

- `Highlights of the 2022 Annual Poverty Indicators Survey (APIS): Education` gives a household-level regional outcome snapshot for 2022: in MIMAROPA, 97.2 percent of families with children aged 6-11 had children in Grade 1-6, and 90.1 percent of families with children aged 12-15 had children in junior high school. This partly fills the “household/regional outcomes” gap, though it is regional and not a national Marcos-specific causal estimate.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Highlights of the 2022 Annual Poverty Indicators Survey (APIS): Education","publisher":"Philippine Statistics Authority, MIMAROPA regional office","publication_date":"2024-04-03","url":"https://rssomimaropa.psa.gov.ph/content/highlights-2022-annual-poverty-indicators-survey-apis-education","claim":"In MIMAROPA, 97.2% of families with children aged 6-11 had children in Grade 1-6 in 2022, and 90.1% of families with children aged 12-15 had children in junior high school.","locator":"lines 175-185","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official PSA regional APIS release using household survey data, with explicit percentages and year reference.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective 2024 reporting on 2022 household survey data.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect: these are regional household outcomes during Marcos's first year, but they are not uniquely attributable to his choices without additional causal isolation.","contrary_evidence":["Regional figures do not establish national performance.","They reflect a single region and a household survey, so they are informative but not comprehensive."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.10"]}

- `Highlights of the 2022 Annual Poverty Indicators Survey (APIS): Social Protection Programs` gives another household-level regional snapshot: in MIMAROPA, 24.8 percent of families were beneficiaries of 4Ps, 12.1 percent received AICS, and 84.8 percent had at least one PhilHealth-linked member/dependent/beneficiary. That is useful evidence that social-protection reach was real and measurable at household level.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Highlights of the 2022 Annual Poverty Indicators Survey: Social Protection Programs","publisher":"Philippine Statistics Authority, MIMAROPA regional office","publication_date":"2024-04-17","url":"https://rssomimaropa.psa.gov.ph/content/highlights-2022-annual-poverty-indicators-survey-social-protection-programs","claim":"In MIMAROPA, 24.8% of families were beneficiaries of 4Ps, 12.1% received AICS, and 84.8% had at least one member/dependent/beneficiary of PhilHealth in 2022.","locator":"social assistance and PhilHealth sections in the article","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official PSA regional APIS release grounded in household survey data and reporting multiple program participation rates.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective 2024 reporting on 2022 household survey data.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect: the figures reflect program reach under the Marcos period, but the article does not isolate his personal contribution from inherited program design and regional administration.","contrary_evidence":["Regional reach is not the same as quality or effectiveness of delivery.","The data are regional and do not show whether Marcos personally improved access beyond inherited systems."],"lenses":["8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}

- `Percentage of Filipino Families Classified as Poor Declined to 10.9 percent in 2023` is a broader trajectory check. PSA says poverty incidence among families was 10.9 percent in 2023, mean per capita income of the second decile rose 22.9 percent, and poverty incidence among families fell 2.3 points from 2021. This is post-window follow-through, not pure 2022 evidence, but it helps test whether the 2022 program translated into improved household conditions.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Percentage of Filipino Families Classified as Poor Declined to 10.9 percent in 2023","publisher":"Philippine Statistics Authority","publication_date":"2024-07-22","url":"https://psa.gov.ph/content/percentage-filipino-families-classified-poor-declined-109-percent-2023","claim":"PSA reported that poverty incidence among families was 10.9% in 2023, and that the mean per capita income of families near the poverty line increased 22.9% from 2021 to 2023, producing a 2.3 percentage-point decline in family poverty incidence.","locator":"lines 344-351","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"National statistical release with explicit poverty and income figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective follow-through beyond 2022; useful for trajectory but not a direct within-year 2022 outcome measure.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Marcos-era policy and budget choices, but also heavily shaped by post-pandemic recovery and price dynamics.","contrary_evidence":["This is 2023 outcome data, so it cannot be treated as a clean measure of 2022-only execution.","The release itself notes the decline is explained partly by threshold and income changes over time."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.10"]}

- `11 out of 18 Regions Recorded Significant Decreases in Poverty Incidence in 2023` strengthens the regional trajectory picture. PSA reported that 11 of 18 regions saw significant declines from 2021 to 2023, while a few regions worsened or were mixed. This is still follow-through evidence, but it is the best regional distributional check I found in this round.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"11 out of 18 Regions Recorded Significant Decreases in Poverty Incidence in 2023","publisher":"Philippine Statistics Authority","publication_date":"2024-08-15","url":"https://psa.gov.ph/statistics/poverty/node/1684064929","claim":"PSA reported that 11 of 18 regions recorded statistically significant decreases in family poverty incidence in 2023 from 2021, while some regions saw increases or only non-significant downtrends.","locator":"lines 337-352","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"National statistics release with explicit regional poverty comparisons and significance language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective 2024 regional follow-through on the 2022-2023 trajectory.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Marcos-era macro and social-policy choices, but also affected by inherited recovery patterns and regional heterogeneity.","contrary_evidence":["This is a 2023 comparison against 2021, not a direct 2022 administration-only effect.","Some regions worsened, so the regional picture is mixed."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.8","8B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I targeted DBM budget-execution records, PSA household/outcome releases, and COA audit sources.
- The DBM NCA-utilization Excel behind the official page hit a cache miss, so I used the BusinessWorld report summarizing DBM data instead.
- A PSA regional social-protection page was blocked by 403 on direct open, but the search snippet was sufficient to recover the underlying APIS figures.
- COA pages mostly exposed repository/FAQ material, not a Marcos-specific 2022 audit finding that would materially improve the anti-corruption/capture gap.
- I did not find a clean 2022 COA or procurement anomaly report tied directly to Marcos that would move the competence judgment beyond the existing EO 1 / PCGG conflict evidence already in the index.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Budget execution: partly resolved. There is now a concrete late-2022 utilization signal, but not a program-by-program rollout record.
- Household/regional outcomes: partly resolved. National 2023 poverty/income data and 2022 APIS regional snapshots improve coverage, but the national 2022 household picture is still thinner than the macro data.
- Anti-corruption/capture: not recoverable in this round. I did not find a direct 2022 audit/procurement finding that materially outperforms the existing conflict-of-interest and institutional-restructuring evidence already in the index.
